{"title":"HumanFish — Predicting Who Actually Wins a Human Chess Game","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/974119055/0/marginalrevolution~HumanFish-Predicting-Who-Actually-Wins-a-Human-Chess-Game.html","date":1795721357000,"content":"<blockquote>\r\n<p>The eval bar on a chess broadcast answers the wrong question. It tells you what happens if two 3600-Elo engines play perfectly with infinite time. What you actually want to know while watching humans play is: <strong>who is going to win from here, how likely is a draw, and why?</strong></p>\r\n<p><strong>HumanFish</strong> is an open hybrid model trained on <strong>550,001 tournament positions (91,720 human 2400–2900 games)</strong> combining <strong>Stockfish 19</strong>, <strong>Leela Chess Zero</strong>, a grandmaster move predictor (<strong>DSC-150M</strong>), and <strong>live tournament clocks</strong>. It outputs calibrated <code>White Win / Draw / Black Win</code> odds—and shows the concrete moves behind every disagreement right on the board.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>I have long wanted such a thing, here is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://humanfish.vercel.app/methodology\" target=\"_blank\">the link</a>.  Built by the well-known <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://chatgpt.com/s/t_6b088852d0288191be4244b86326777b\" target=\"_blank\">book reviewer Patrick Collison</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/11/humanfish-predicting-who-actually-wins-a-human-chess-game.html\">HumanFish — Predicting Who Actually Wins a Human Chess Game</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/974119055/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/